## Is psoriasis a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. A long-term skin condition that affects everyday activities, such as using your hands, sleeping or getting dressed, can be covered. The law also treats a **severe disfigurement** as having a substantial effect, even if it does not affect what you can do. Psoriatic arthritis is also likely to count.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include:

- time off for appointments and phototherapy
- suitable uniform or gloves, if work irritates your skin
- flexible hours during flare-ups
- adapted equipment, if psoriatic arthritis affects your hands

You should not face discrimination because of how your skin looks.

## Benefits

Most people with psoriasis will not qualify for PIP. Severe psoriasis, especially with **psoriatic arthritis**, may affect activities such as **preparing food**, **washing**, **dressing** and **moving around**, or **managing treatments** if you need help applying creams.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.
